1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,650
Khwarazm, on the far northern edge of the
empire. An old general who has served

2
00:00:05,650 --> 00:00:10,808
three rulers is still alive. Somewhere on the
road from the sultan's court, a secret

3
00:00:10,808 --> 00:00:14,860
order is travelling toward him. Its purpose is
to have him killed.

4
00:00:25,210 --> 00:00:30,363
Welcome to The Honest Historian. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani, and this is Folio Seven. Today's

5
00:00:30,363 --> 00:00:35,455
story is about an old general who served three
rulers faithfully, and about the day

6
00:00:35,455 --> 00:00:38,890
his own sultan signed a secret order to have
him killed.

7
00:00:39,670 --> 00:00:45,302
The order failed. And what the old general did
next is the reason Beyhaqi remembered

8
00:00:45,302 --> 00:00:45,570
him.

9
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:06,510
His name was Altuntash. In Turkish, the name
means something like golden stone.

10
00:01:07,290 --> 00:01:12,589
Like many of the great commanders of Ghazni,
Altuntash did not begin life as a lord.

11
00:01:12,589 --> 00:01:17,888
He began as a slave soldier. In this world,
that was not the end of a career. It was

12
00:01:17,888 --> 00:01:19,150
often the beginning.

13
00:01:19,430 --> 00:01:24,226
Young Turks from the steppe were bought,
trained as soldiers, and the best of them

14
00:01:24,226 --> 00:01:27,150
could rise to command armies and govern
provinces.

15
00:01:27,930 --> 00:01:33,102
Altuntash served Sebuktegin, the founder of
the dynasty. Then he served Sebuktegin's

16
00:01:33,102 --> 00:01:37,967
son, the great Mahmud of Ghazni. He fought the
Turkish kings who came down from

17
00:01:37,967 --> 00:01:41,230
beyond the Oxus river. He governed the city of
Herat.

18
00:01:41,510 --> 00:01:46,880
And in the year ten seventeen, when Mahmud
conquered the rich land of Khwarazm, far

19
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:52,250
to the north, on the lower Oxus near the Aral
Sea, he gave it to Altuntash to rule.

20
00:01:53,030 --> 00:01:59,438
From then on, Altuntash carried an old and
proud title. Khwarazmshah. The king of

21
00:01:59,438 --> 00:02:00,150
Khwarazm.

22
00:02:00,930 --> 00:02:05,855
For years he guarded that frontier. He kept
the wild horsemen of the steppe from

23
00:02:05,855 --> 00:02:10,780
raiding the farms and the towns. He was far
from the court, rich, respected and,

24
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:11,950
above all, trusted.

25
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:30,510
Then, in the year ten thirty, Mahmud died.

26
00:02:31,290 --> 00:02:35,935
You know what happened next, if you heard our
earlier folios. Mahmud's two sons,

27
00:02:35,935 --> 00:02:40,696
Muhammad and Masud, fought over the throne.
Masud won. His brother was blinded and

28
00:02:40,696 --> 00:02:45,284
locked away. And the new sultan began to sort
the men he had inherited from his

29
00:02:45,284 --> 00:02:45,690
father.

30
00:02:46,470 --> 00:02:51,449
Some of them he kept. Some he destroyed. In
Folio One, we watched the old vizier

31
00:02:51,449 --> 00:02:56,552
Hasanak climb a gallows. Other great men of
Mahmud's time were arrested, one after

32
00:02:56,552 --> 00:02:57,050
another.

33
00:02:57,830 --> 00:03:03,093
Now put yourself in Altuntash's place, far
away in Khwarazm. He is old. He has an

34
00:03:03,093 --> 00:03:08,551
army. He has money. He served the father, not
the son. And every month, news arrives

35
00:03:08,551 --> 00:03:11,670
of another old servant of Mahmud who has
fallen.

36
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:17,587
And put yourself in Masud's place. A powerful
general sits on the northern edge of

37
00:03:17,587 --> 00:03:22,473
the empire, with his own army, his own
treasury, and a famous old title. He is

38
00:03:22,473 --> 00:03:24,290
loyal, people say. But is he?

39
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:30,020
This is the kind of fear that grows in the
dark. And at Masud's court, there was a

40
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:31,770
man who knew how to water it.

41
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:50,750
His name will be familiar. Bu Sahl Zawzani.

42
00:03:51,530 --> 00:03:56,553
In Folio Five, we met him as the clever,
bitter rival of Beyhaqi's teacher, Bu Nasr

43
00:03:56,553 --> 00:04:01,333
Moshkan. In Folio Six, we heard him write a
poem in praise of the sultan's lion

44
00:04:01,333 --> 00:04:06,356
hunt. Bu Sahl was close to Masud, and he had a
gift for telling the sultan what the

45
00:04:06,356 --> 00:04:07,990
sultan half wanted to hear.

46
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:13,990
Beyhaqi gives a whole section of his book a
title that says it plainly. Bu Sahl's

47
00:04:13,990 --> 00:04:16,310
mischief in the matter of Altuntash.

48
00:04:17,090 --> 00:04:21,856
Bu Sahl pressed the idea that Altuntash could
not be trusted. That he was too

49
00:04:21,856 --> 00:04:26,932
strong, too far away, too independent. That it
would be wiser to remove him before

50
00:04:26,932 --> 00:04:28,170
he became a problem.

51
00:04:28,950 --> 00:04:31,110
And Masud listened.

52
00:04:31,890 --> 00:04:37,678
A secret order was sent to Khwarazm, to a
commander there named Qaid Malanjuq. Its

53
00:04:37,678 --> 00:04:40,290
purpose was to have Altuntash killed.

54
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:46,227
Stop for a moment and think about that. Not a
trial. Not a summons to court. A quiet

55
00:04:46,227 --> 00:04:51,323
death, arranged from far away, for a man who
had served the dynasty for most of his

56
00:04:51,323 --> 00:04:51,630
life.

57
00:05:06,910 --> 00:05:08,330
It did not work.

58
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:13,632
The secret did not stay secret, and the plot
fell apart. The next section of

59
00:05:13,632 --> 00:05:18,631
Beyhaqi's book has a short and grim title. The
killing of Qaid Malanjuq. The man who

60
00:05:18,631 --> 00:05:23,510
was meant to strike the blow did not survive
it. Altuntash was alive, and he knew.

61
00:05:24,290 --> 00:05:29,139
Now we come to the dangerous moment. An old
general with an army has just learned

62
00:05:29,139 --> 00:05:33,270
that his sultan tried to murder him. What
would you expect him to do?

63
00:05:34,050 --> 00:05:39,112
Break away, perhaps. Declare Khwarazm his own.
Make an alliance with the Turkish

64
00:05:39,112 --> 00:05:44,427
kings across the river, the enemies of Ghazni.
Many men in his place would have done

65
00:05:44,427 --> 00:05:45,250
exactly that.

66
00:05:46,030 --> 00:05:50,777
And at court, they knew it. Historians who
have studied these events agree on the

67
00:05:50,777 --> 00:05:55,642
fear that now ran through Masud's circle. If
Altuntash rebelled, the whole northern

68
00:05:55,642 --> 00:05:56,990
frontier could be lost.

69
00:05:57,770 --> 00:06:00,350
So Masud did two things.

70
00:06:01,130 --> 00:06:05,644
First, he turned on the man who had given him
the idea. Bu Sahl Zawzani fell from

71
00:06:05,644 --> 00:06:10,102
favour. He was arrested, and his wealth and
his servants were taken from him. It

72
00:06:10,102 --> 00:06:14,728
would not last forever. Bu Sahl was a
survivor, and in time he came back. But for a

73
00:06:14,728 --> 00:06:17,570
while, the clever courtier paid for his
cleverness.

74
00:06:18,350 --> 00:06:21,430
Second, Masud wrote to Altuntash.

75
00:06:22,210 --> 00:06:26,857
Beyhaqi preserves the letter in his book. That
is one of the great treasures of this

76
00:06:26,857 --> 00:06:31,504
history. Beyhaqi worked in the royal chancery,
the office where the sultan's letters

77
00:06:31,504 --> 00:06:36,041
were written, and he copied real documents
into his story. So we are not reading a

78
00:06:36,041 --> 00:06:38,530
historian's guess about what the sultan said.

79
00:06:38,810 --> 00:06:41,910
We are reading what the court actually sent.

80
00:06:42,690 --> 00:06:47,527
Its purpose is clear. It was meant to calm an
old servant of the state and to win

81
00:06:47,527 --> 00:06:52,065
back his trust. It is the sound of a powerful
man trying very hard to undo a

82
00:06:52,065 --> 00:06:55,170
mistake, without ever quite saying the word
mistake.

83
00:07:10,450 --> 00:07:11,850
And Altuntash?

84
00:07:12,630 --> 00:07:14,270
He did not rebel.

85
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:20,445
He stayed in Khwarazm. He kept his title. And
he went on serving the sultan who had

86
00:07:20,445 --> 00:07:22,070
tried to have him killed.

87
00:07:22,850 --> 00:07:27,106
We should be honest here. We cannot see inside
his heart. Perhaps it was pure

88
00:07:27,106 --> 00:07:31,529
loyalty to the house he had served since he
was a young man. Perhaps it was cool

89
00:07:31,529 --> 00:07:35,896
good sense. He was old, and a rebellion is a
young man's gamble. Perhaps it was

90
00:07:35,896 --> 00:07:38,770
both. Beyhaqi lets his actions speak, and so
will I.

91
00:07:39,550 --> 00:07:42,510
Because the real test was still coming.

92
00:07:57,790 --> 00:08:03,000
In the year ten thirty two, Masud decided to
strike at Ali Tegin, a Turkish king who

93
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,210
ruled beyond the Oxus, in the lands around
Bukhara and Samarkand. Ali Tegin had long

94
00:08:08,210 --> 00:08:13,172
been a thorn in Ghazni's side. Masud made an
alliance with another Turkish ruler

95
00:08:13,172 --> 00:08:16,770
against him. And he ordered the army of
Khwarazm to march.

96
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:19,430
He ordered Altuntash.

97
00:08:20,210 --> 00:08:25,318
Think of it. The same sultan who had sent a
secret death sentence now sent an order

98
00:08:25,318 --> 00:08:28,210
to go to war for him. And the old general
went.

99
00:08:28,990 --> 00:08:33,839
Altuntash crossed into the lands beyond the
river and met Ali Tegin's army at a

100
00:08:33,839 --> 00:08:38,872
place called Dabusiyya, on the road between
Bukhara and Samarkand. Beyhaqi gives a

101
00:08:38,872 --> 00:08:42,370
whole section of his history to the planning
of this war.

102
00:08:43,150 --> 00:08:48,316
The battle was hard, and it decided nothing.
Neither side broke the other. But

103
00:08:48,316 --> 00:08:50,170
Altuntash was badly wounded.

104
00:08:50,950 --> 00:08:56,215
He did not lose his army. He held it together,
and pulled it back out of danger. But

105
00:08:56,215 --> 00:09:00,790
the wound was mortal. Not long afterwards,
Altuntash, Khwarazmshah, died.

106
00:09:01,570 --> 00:09:05,530
He died in the service of the man who had
tried to kill him.

107
00:09:20,810 --> 00:09:26,000
Now a new figure steps forward. Remember his
name, because he will matter a great

108
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:27,730
deal in the folios to come.

109
00:09:28,510 --> 00:09:34,139
Altuntash had a chief minister, his steward
and right hand, a man from Shiraz named

110
00:09:34,139 --> 00:09:35,970
Ahmad, son of Abd al Samad.

111
00:09:36,750 --> 00:09:41,541
An army far from home, whose commander is
dying or dead, is in terrible danger. If

112
00:09:41,541 --> 00:09:46,390
the enemy learns of it, they attack. If the
soldiers learn of it, they may scatter.

113
00:09:46,390 --> 00:09:50,830
It is said that Ahmad kept his master's death
quiet until the army was safe.

114
00:09:51,110 --> 00:09:55,910
What we know for certain is that he made peace
with Ali Tegin, and that he brought

115
00:09:55,910 --> 00:09:56,730
the army home.

116
00:09:57,510 --> 00:10:02,655
It was a quiet piece of work, done with a cool
head and no glory. And the sultan

117
00:10:02,655 --> 00:10:03,170
noticed.

118
00:10:03,950 --> 00:10:08,889
Not long afterwards, Masud needed a new
vizier, the chief minister of the whole

119
00:10:08,889 --> 00:10:13,765
empire. Beyhaqi tells us whom he chose. Khwaja
Ahmad, son of Abd al Samad. The

120
00:10:13,765 --> 00:10:19,017
steward of Khwarazm became the first minister
of Ghazni. He would stand beside Masud

121
00:10:19,017 --> 00:10:20,830
through the hard years ahead.

122
00:10:21,610 --> 00:10:27,599
And in Khwarazm, Altuntash's son Harun took
charge of his father's land. But that is

123
00:10:27,599 --> 00:10:29,310
a story for another day.

124
00:10:44,590 --> 00:10:49,590
Let me jump forward eight years, to show you
why this story stayed with Beyhaqi.

125
00:10:50,370 --> 00:10:55,175
By the year ten forty, everything was falling
apart. The new enemies we will meet

126
00:10:55,175 --> 00:11:00,039
next week were winning. Masud was stubborn,
suspicious, and listening to the wrong

127
00:11:00,039 --> 00:11:00,810
people again.

128
00:11:01,590 --> 00:11:06,778
In one scene from those last days, Beyhaqi
shows us the vizier speaking privately to

129
00:11:06,778 --> 00:11:11,348
none other than Bu Sahl Zawzani. That vizier
was Ahmad, the old steward of

130
00:11:11,348 --> 00:11:14,930
Altuntash. And he says a sentence that Beyhaqi
wrote down.

131
00:11:15,710 --> 00:11:18,370
In Persian, it is very short.

132
00:11:19,150 --> 00:11:21,950
In soltan na an ast ke bud.

133
00:11:22,730 --> 00:11:25,610
This sultan is not the man he was.

134
00:11:26,390 --> 00:11:31,134
I find it hard to hear that line without
thinking of the old general who died at

135
00:11:31,134 --> 00:11:36,056
Dabusiyya. Ahmad had seen, with his own eyes,
what loyalty looked like. He had seen

136
00:11:36,056 --> 00:11:41,037
a man stay faithful to a master who did not
deserve it. And now, years later, he was

137
00:11:41,037 --> 00:11:43,350
watching that same master lose his way.

138
00:11:58,630 --> 00:12:02,510
So what does Beyhaqi want us to take from
Altuntash?

139
00:12:03,290 --> 00:12:08,235
He does not preach. He shows. On one side, a
sultan who listened to a whisper, and

140
00:12:08,235 --> 00:12:13,302
signed a secret order in the dark. On the
other, an old servant who had every reason

141
00:12:13,302 --> 00:12:14,930
to betray him, and did not.

142
00:12:15,710 --> 00:12:20,738
And between them, Bu Sahl, the man who
whispered. Notice that Beyhaqi, who disliked

143
00:12:20,738 --> 00:12:25,766
him, does not invent anything to blacken his
name. He simply sets down what Bu Sahl

144
00:12:25,766 --> 00:12:28,310
advised, and what it cost. That is enough.

145
00:12:29,090 --> 00:12:33,733
In Beyhaqi's world, power is fragile. It does
not depend only on armies and

146
00:12:33,733 --> 00:12:38,810
treasure. It depends on trust. And trust, once
a king throws it away, is very hard

147
00:12:38,810 --> 00:12:41,410
to buy back, even with a beautiful letter.

148
00:12:56,690 --> 00:13:01,984
If there is one lesson here, it is this: a
ruler who listens to whispers against his

149
00:13:01,984 --> 00:13:05,450
most loyal men will one day find that he has
none left.

150
00:13:06,230 --> 00:13:11,177
Next week, a new people rides out of the
steppe. We meet the Seljuqs, the nomads at

151
00:13:11,177 --> 00:13:15,230
the edge of the empire, who will change the
history of Iran forever.

152
00:13:16,010 --> 00:13:20,758
If you enjoyed this story, follow the show so
you don't miss the next one, and share

153
00:13:20,758 --> 00:13:25,394
it with someone who loves history. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani. This episode was retold from

154
00:13:25,394 --> 00:13:30,142
the Persian original of the History of
Beyhaqi, and narrated with an AI rendering of

155
00:13:30,142 --> 00:13:32,290
my own voice. Thank you for listening.

